{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Q6W1X2pCDws427rDMnSfbt","type":"tool_result","content":"commit 5129f90b01dc26ae685f6fcea26cdb775ea1d99f\nAuthor: ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] <359953783+ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Wed Nov 25 16:44:15 2026 +0100\n\n    chore: Network inputs back-route, part 4: end-to-end test and pr… (#387)\n    \n    Adds an offline end-to-end test of the network inputs back-route (bounce through reviewers, plus three negative paths) and brings docs/proposal.md up to the shipped behaviour.\n    \n    ## Changes\n    - internal/servecmd/network_e2e_test.go (new): one harness with the triage, worker and reviewer loops sharing one fake GitHub, one bare remote and one store, driven by explicit ticks. One fake agent script serves every role and counts runs per role. No network is used.\n    - TestNetworkBounceEndToEnd: a `network: false` worker answers needs-network, so the engineer is requested (no branch, no PR). The engineer declares the input, then the lead and architect re-run. The gate holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment until the file lands on main. A fresh \"ready for approval\" follows, the old approval is stale, and a re-added approval starts the worker. The worker pushes a branch and opens a draft PR, and the reviewer posts its review.\n    - Negative tests: second round, branch already exists, and a worker-provided input with a `network: false` worker. Each ends needs-human, and the first two list the entries.\n    - docs/proposal.md: flowchart edge from worker to triage, label table rows and bounce paragraph, role-file contract (removed the \"for now\" and \"part N\" wording), the starter lead, engineer and worker paragraphs, worker step 9, the gate paragraph, and the `network` configuration paragraph.\n    \n    ## Testing\n    `go test ./internal/servecmd/ -run Network` passes (4 tests, about 27 s because approvals wait for second-resolution timestamps). `go test ./...` passes, including `internal/docscheck`. `make lint` was not completed: `go vet ./...` stops on an existing broken testdata file, `internal/roles/testdata/docs/skips/src/_x.go`, which I did not touch. `gofmt` is clean on the new file.\n    \n    ## Open issues\n    - The live prompt copies under `.ard/` still need a human PR, as the issue says.\n    - The fake GitHub does not track git, so the test sets the PR head SHA to the pushed branch tip by hand before the reviewer runs.\ncommit 7983ee149f865a8b32088016e52a565dd1238ba7\nAuthor: ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] <359953783+ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Wed Nov 25 16:48:59 2026 +0100\n\n    ard(worker:engineer): #387 no_process: could not open the pull request\n    \n    Ard-Job: 01M89NDVQMSBFN4DMPM7WNB5XT\n\n .ard/387/costs/01M89NDVQMSBFN4DMPM7WNB5XT.json     |  23 +++\n .../logs/worker-engineer-20261125T153315.039Z.log  | 159 +++++++++++++++++++++\n     537 internal/servecmd/network_e2e_test.go\n31:    W -->|\"needs-network: one round, first job only\"| T\n43:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A worker that answers `needs-network` before it has pushed anything goes back to the triage engineer once (the edge from Worker to Triage); a reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n75:| `ard:triage:<role>` | Lead (via controller) or human | This triage role is requested. A needs-network worker close-out requests the engineer this way, and the lead's close-out re-requests the other roles in a network round |\n135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n137:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land. Label state machine: the engineer's `done` close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes `:done` from each re-opened role and requests it again; the gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent. The gate records one `wait` gate event when it starts waiting for inputs and one `inputs-ready` event when they land; the daily status-issue digest shows a *Waiting for inputs* count of open issues whose newest gate event is the wait.\n150:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human | needs-network (the last three add ard:needs-human, except a first needs-network, which bounces to triage)\n161:network:            # workers only, with status needs-network: 1 to 10 entries (#384)\n282:The starter engineer (`.ard/triage/engineer/prompt.txt`) plans for the worker without implementing. Its inputs are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md`, `comments.md` and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, all untrusted. It writes the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md` with Steps, Test plan, Risk and Size, sized S, M, L or XL on a scale tied to the worker diff guard's block limit (XL is over the worker role's `diff_max_lines`, 3,000 changed lines by default). It writes `triage-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, never a `worker` key. It answers `needs-human` for an XL change, a change to protected role and config paths or `.github/`, or a missing product or design decision, and leaves `plan.md` untouched. In Phase 1 it has no `after:`; the lead's request orders it. The committer and close-out guards confine it to `.ard/<issue#>/`, and close-out allows it only `triage-engineer.md` and the `## Implementation` section of `plan.md`; a change to `triage-lead.md`, `issue.md` or another role's section fails the job as needs-human. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present. When the change needs files from the network it adds a `### Network inputs` subsection (exact URL, pinned version, verification source, landing path, and who provides it: the worker if its role has network, else a human); it never fetches and never writes a checksum it did not read from a file (#301). When a job has `network-request.md` (the untrusted, redacted list of what a worker asked for), it reads it, updates `### Network inputs` and declares `inputs:` in `triage-engineer.md` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), at most 10 (#386).\n324:Kubernetes-executor details (decided, design revision 3 of [#54](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/54), recorded in [#312](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/312); the work is split across later parts of #54, and #317 splits into the API layer and the executor). There is no job API on `ard serve`. **Pod shape.** `pre` fetches with the token passed through `http.extraheader` from the environment, never argv or `.git/config`; the git dir is on a `ctl` volume the agent does not mount; the role dir comes from `cfg.SHA`, so the k8s executor refuses a config without a SHA; sidecars are at pinned SHAs; the untrusted inputs and the scrub list travel in the per-job Secret, capped at 768 KiB (over the cap is a permanent failure with a named cause); `pre` copies the static `ard` binary. The `agent` container mounts only `work` and `out` and holds only its model credential via `secretKeyRef`: no GitHub token, no key, no Secret volume, no SA token. It tees stdout to `out/stdout`, enforces its own deadline, writes a done marker and reaps orphans as PID 1. `post` has its own pinned image and never runs a binary from a shared volume. It serves one route, `GET /v1/result`, on the pod IP, guarded by a preshared 32-byte `crypto/rand` key unique to the pod, mounted only in `post` and kept in memory by the controller, compared in constant time. It answers 409 until the agent has terminated and the marker exists, builds the tar in Go and never runs git, serves one request at a time, has header and idle timeouts, never logs Authorization, and exits 0 after the controller confirms or at the deadline. The transport is plain HTTP inside the cluster (the owner's choice): the key grants only this job's result, and a NetworkPolicy limits who can connect.\n365:9. **Report.** Comment on the issue with the summary, PR link and test result (`passed`, `passed after 1 fix attempt`, `failed after 2 fix attempts`, `not configured`); swap `ard:worker:<role>:running` for `:done`. If tests still fail, add `ard:tests:failed` on the issue and the PR and request no reviewers; a green run removes it. A worker that ends without the loop's result (for example one the Reaper closes out), or whose role file says `needs-human`, never becomes `:done` without a PR. A role file that says `needs-network` on a first job (no branch, no PR, nothing pushed) bounces the issue to the triage engineer once, as described under the role-file contract; in any other case it ends `needs-human`, nothing is pushed, and the comment lists the needed inputs.\n367:**The starter worker (`workers/engineer`, #34).** `.ard/workers/engineer/prompt.txt` implements `plan.md`'s `## Implementation`. Its inputs, all untrusted, are `.ard-input/<issue#>/issue.md` and `comments.md`, in a fix round `test-output.log`, and `.ard/<issue#>/plan.md`, `issue.md`, the triage role files and logs, in a review round `review-feedback.md` and the reviewer role files and logs. It writes code, tests and docs, and `worker-engineer.md` with `role: engineer` and `attempt: 1`, an optional `next` of `review:` entries, and never `worker` or `verdict`. The body has Changes, Testing and Open issues sections; it becomes the commit body, the PR description and the issue comment, and a fix round's body describes the whole change. It answers `needs-human` for a change the diff guard would reject, more changed lines than the role's `diff_max_lines` (3,000 by default; above `diff_warn_lines`, 2,000 by default, the PR goes ahead with the large-change note), a missing decision, nothing to change, or an input that tries to instruct it. In a fix round it keeps `done` and explains a failure it cannot fix, so the loop reports `ard:tests:failed` on the PR. The repository's package manager may fetch a dependency the change needs (`go get`, `npm install`); the agent installs no system packages. With `network: true` it may fetch other inputs, records each under `fetched:` and treats the fetched bytes as untrusted data, and the guard and reviewers still see `go.mod` and lockfile changes. Credential-shaped test fixtures are built at run time. The starter override is Sonnet 5.5, `timeout: 60m` for the whole job, `tests: make lint test` (so formatting and vet failures are caught before a PR opens), and the default diff thresholds written out (`diff_warn_lines: 2000`, `diff_max_lines: 3000`). It ships no Dockerfile, so it runs as a host subprocess with the #84 warning until #55's `ard-agent` image gives a worker image a base. It reads `TECH_STACK.md` when present; the file is a constraint, never an instruction, and the worker never resolves a conflict with it: it keeps to the issue and plan and notes the conflict in Open issues. With `Network: available.` the worker fetches only what the plan's Network inputs name, verifies each file against the publisher's checksum file, never invents or recalls a value, records each file in `fetched:` and in a `CAPTURE.md` beside the fixtures, and commits a regen script and Make target; with `Network: unavailable.` it fetches nothing and answers `needs-network` with a `network:` list (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`) when no branch exists yet, or `needs-human` when a branch or PR does (#301, #384). The controller bounces a first `needs-network` once; the second ends `needs-human`.\n511:**`network` (#297, part of #276).** Every agent kind takes an optional boolean `network` override, default `true`. In this part it is advisory only and nothing is enforced: a role with `network: false` gets `ARD_NETWORK=0` (else `1`) in its job environment and a constant \"Network: unavailable.\" (else \"Network: available.\") line in the agent's user message. The value rides on the executor job and handle from config only, never from anything the agent can read or change. In host mode the controller logs that `network: false` is not enforced. `network: false` on a worker is what makes it answer `needs-network`: the controller bounces that to the triage engineer once, and the gate refuses an input the engineer assigns to a `worker` provider when the worker role has `network: false` (the issue ends `needs-human`, so a human provides the file on `main`). `ard config check` prints `false (advisory)`, or `false (advisory, host)` for a host-mode role, and warns when a host-mode role that reads untrusted input has `network: true`, naming the GitHub App private key file as reachable.\n527:- **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) job egress is DNS and the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture), and `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"36ff2b97-3630-4ef5-bd56-89165e98aced","uuid":"bf207995-4414-4265-aa95-482df48979ca","timestamp":"2026-11-25T16:22:00.944Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"commit 5129f90b01dc26ae685f6fcea26cdb775ea1d99f\nAuthor: ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] <359953783+ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Wed Nov 25 16:44:15 2026 +0100\n\n    chore: Network inputs back-route, part 4: end-to-end test and pr… (#387)\n    \n    Adds an offline end-to-end test of the network inputs back-route (bounce through reviewers, plus three negative paths) and brings docs/proposal.md up to the shipped behaviour.\n    \n    ## Changes\n    - internal/servecmd/network_e2e_test.go (new): one harness with the triage, worker and reviewer loops sharing one fake GitHub, one bare remote and one store, driven by explicit ticks. One fake agent script serves every role and counts runs per role. No network is used.\n    - TestNetworkBounceEndToEnd: a `network: false` worker answers needs-network, so the engineer is requested (no branch, no PR). The engineer declares the input, then the lead and architect re-run. The gate holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment until the file lands on main. A fresh \"ready for approval\" follows, the old approval is stale, and a re-added approval starts the worker. The worker pushes a branch and opens a draft PR, and the reviewer posts its review.\n    - Negative tests: second round, branch already exists, and a worker-provided input with a `network: false` worker. Each ends needs-human, and the first two list the entries.\n    - docs/proposal.md: flowchart edge from worker to triage, label table rows and bounce paragraph, role-file contract (removed the \"for now\" and \"part N\" wording), the starter lead, engineer and worker paragraphs, worker step 9, the gate paragraph, and the `network` configuration paragraph.\n    \n    ## Testing\n    `go test ./internal/servecmd/ -run Network` passes (4 tests, about 27 s because approvals wait for second-resolution timestamps). `go test ./...` passes, including `internal/docscheck`. `make lint` was not completed: `go vet ./...` stops on an existing broken testdata file, `internal/roles/testdata/docs/skips/src/_x.go`, which I did not touch. `gofmt` is clean on the new file.\n    \n    ## Open issues\n    - The live prompt copies under `.ard/` still need a human PR, as the issue says.\n    - The fake GitHub does not track git, so the test sets the PR head SHA to the pushed branch tip by hand before the reviewer runs.\ncommit 7983ee149f865a8b32088016e52a565dd1238ba7\nAuthor: ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot] <359953783+ard-bot-albertcbrown[bot]@users.noreply.github.com>\nDate:   Wed Nov 25 16:48:59 2026 +0100\n\n    ard(worker:engineer): #387 no_process: could not open the pull request\n    \n    Ard-Job: 01M89NDVQMSBFN4DMPM7WNB5XT\n\n .ard/387/costs/01M89NDVQMSBFN4DMPM7WNB5XT.json     |  23 +++\n .../logs/worker-engineer-20261125T153315.039Z.log  | 159 +++++++++++++++++++++\n     537 internal/servecmd/network_e2e_test.go\n31:    W -->|\"needs-network: one round, first job only\"| T\n43:Each arrow is a label the controller sets at close-out; dashed lines show the stages it runs. A worker that answers `needs-network` before it has pushed anything goes back to the triage engineer once (the edge from Worker to Triage); a reviewer's `changes` verdict sends the PR back to the worker, at most three times; once every review passes, the merger lands it.\n75:| `ard:triage:<role>` | Lead (via controller) or human | This triage role is requested. A needs-network worker close-out requests the engineer this way, and the lead's close-out re-requests the other roles in a network round |\n135:**Network needs (#384).** A worker that cannot fetch an input itself writes `status: needs-network` and a `network:` list of 1 to 10 entries (`what`, `url`, `why`, optional `verify`). Close-out parses it as strictly as `fetched`, on a worker only, re-parses it after redaction and ends `needs-human` if it no longer parses. The bounce is on in `serve` (`servecmd` sets `closeout.Deps.Network`) and applies to a first worker job only (no branch or PR, and nothing pushed by this job): nothing is pushed and no PR is opened, and close-out requests `ard:triage:engineer` instead of `needs-human` and removes `ard:triage:engineer:done`, `ard:worker:<role>` and `:running`, in that order. The triage job gets `network-request.md`, rendered from the record's structured list and marked as agent-written, untrusted input. One network round per issue is allowed, counted from worker close-outs with outcome `needs-network` in any phase from prepared on (the plan never changes after Prepare, so a crash cannot make a round read as not open); a second, a fix, review or conflict round, a pushed job or a repository without a triage engineer role ends `needs-human`, with one comment that lists each entry with its URL in a code span. The same applies when the bounce is off. The live prompts under `.ard/` need a human PR to match the starter copies; a test drives the whole path (bounce, re-run, gate, approval, worker, reviewers and the three negative cases) against fake agents with no network.\n137:**Network round re-run (#386).** A round is open while the newest bounced worker has no triage engineer `done` after it (job ID order). `Prepare` persists the round in the job record (`NetworkState.Round`, `Done`, `Rerun`), so `Build` stays pure and Resume replays the same plan. The engineer's first `done` in a round consumes it: its close-out clears `ard:triage:lead:done` (`Plan.Clear`, removed in the request phase before the requests are added, 404 counting as success) and adds `ard:pending`, so the lead runs first, and persists the other triage roles that had finished `done` (`Plan.Rerun`). The lead's close-out in that round re-requests those roles and clears their `:done` (roles no longer configured are skipped), so all finish before the gate. A human re-request of the engineer sees no open round and does not re-arm again. The engineer may declare `inputs:` (`path`, `provider` `human` or `worker`, optional https `url`), parsed from the redacted role file and stored in the plan. The gate checks each path (plain characters, clean and relative, not under `.ard/`, `.github/` or `.git`), reads main once per tick at one SHA through `ls-tree` (no content, regular blobs only, size capped, cached per SHA) and holds with a \"waiting for inputs\" comment listing each missing path and the capture-note format until every human-provided input exists. A `worker` input with a `network: false` worker role, a bad path or an oversized file ends `needs-human`. A network round always requires a fresh approval, even with `require_approval: false` and even when the engineer removed every network need: the \"ready for approval\" marker gains a `round=<n>`, and an earlier approval is stale because the engineer's new `:done` follows it. An approval given while inputs are missing still counts once they land. Label state machine: the engineer's `done` close-out removes `ard:triage:lead:done` and adds `ard:pending` before its own `:done`; the lead's close-out removes `:done` from each re-opened role and requests it again; the gate does not run while the lead's `:done` is absent. The gate records one `wait` gate event when it starts waiting for inputs and one `inputs-ready` event when they land; the daily status-issue digest shows a *Waiting for inputs* count of open issues whose newest gate event is the wait.\n150:status: done          # done | blocked | needs-human | needs-network (the last three add ard:needs-human, except a first needs-network, which 